      <notesStmt>
        <note type="provenance-warning" xml:lang="eng">
          <p>IMPORTANT. This file replaces the project's former Kanchipuram sample
          corpus (<mentioned>kanchippuranam.xml</mentioned>, <mentioned>km-saiva.xml</mentioned>)
          with a demonstration corpus for the Tiruvilaiyadal Puranam / Halasya Mahatmya
          pair. Two things are real here and one is not:</p>
          <p><emph>Real:</emph> the chapter (படலம்) numbering, the English gist-title for
          each chapter, and the cross-language <mentioned>@corresp</mentioned> alignment
          to <ref target="halasya-mahatmya.xml">halasya-mahatmya.xml</ref> were produced by
          a research pass over secondary sources (English Wikipedia's Tiruvilaiyadal
          Puranam article for padalams 1-38; shaivam.org's English-translation index pages,
          reached via search-result snippets rather than a direct browse, for padalams
          39-64) cross-checked against two independent sources for the 18/30/16 kandam
          split. Only the 44 padalam-adhyaya pairs the research reached high or
          medium-high confidence on are encoded; the remaining ~20 padalams are not
          included at all rather than guessed at — see the alignment note below for the
          excluded ranges.</p>
          <p><emph>Not real:</emph> the <mentioned>&lt;head&gt;</mentioned> titles are
          English glosses from those secondary sources, not the authentic Tamil படலம்
          rubrics, which were not recovered this session. The verse text inside each
          <mentioned>lg</mentioned> is <emph>synthetic placeholder material</emph>, a
          short generic invocatory couplet reused across chapters to exercise the
          encoding model (apparatus, entity references, alignment) — it is not a
          transcription or translation of any padalam's actual content and must not be
          cited as one. See
          <ref target="../../docs/encoding-guidelines.md">encoding guidelines</ref>,
          section "Replacing the sample corpus", for how real verse text should replace
          this scaffolding.</p>
        </note>
        <note type="alignment" xml:lang="eng">
          <p>44 of the traditional 64 padalams are encoded with a
          <mentioned>@corresp</mentioned> link to their matched Halasya Mahatmya adhyaya
          (see each <mentioned>lg/@corresp</mentioned>). Three encoded chapters (padalam 7,
          18, 55) each stand in for a two-padalam episode the research could not split
          further (7-8, 18-19, 55-56 respectively) and are noted individually. Padalams
          not encoded here — 17, 19-20, 23, 25-29, 31-32, 34, 37-38, 54, 57, and 65-64's
          complement outside the matched set — either had no confident Sanskrit match or
          no recoverable title at all; they are absent rather than asserted on a guess.</p>
        </note>
      </notesStmt>
